Securepath Serialisation library
================================

____
ASN.1 DER (de)serialisation framework
____

:toc:

== Info

Values are written through a `serialiser` and read back through a `deserialiser`, both
thin wrappers over a codec (`codec/asn_der`). Types describe themselves with a single
`serialise` function template that works in both directions, dispatched via ADL.

== Basic usage

[source, c++]
....
#include <securepath/serialisation/util.hpp>

my_type value;
securepath::octet_vector der = securepath::serialisation::asn_der_serialise(value);
my_type back = securepath::serialisation::asn_der_deserialise<my_type>(der);
....

Stream variants exist as well: `asn_der_serialise(stream, value)` and
`asn_der_deserialise<T>(stream)` work with anything that has `write`/`read`
(e.g. `octet_vector_stream`).

== Supported types

* `bool`, all integral types (encoded as DER INTEGER; unsigned types promoted to
  `std::uint64_t`, signed to `std::int64_t`), `float`/`double`
* `std::string` (and `char const*` on the serialise side only)
* `octet_vector` (DER OCTET STRING) and the arbitrary-precision `integer`
* `std::optional<T>` — an absent value is simply omitted from the stream; pair it with
  a tag (see below) so the reader can tell which optional is present
* enums (`enum.hpp`) — encoded through their underlying integer
* containers: `std::vector` (`vector.hpp`), `std::deque` (`deque.hpp`), `std::set`
  (`set.hpp`), `std::map` (`map.hpp`) — include the matching header
* `trailing_data` (`types.hpp`) — collects any bytes remaining in the enclosing
  sequence so a newer peer can extend a message without breaking an older reader

== Custom types

Either a member function

[source, c++]
....
#include <securepath/serialisation/sequence.hpp>

struct point {
	int x{};
	int y{};

	template<typename Ar>
	void serialise(Ar& ar) {
		securepath::serialisation::sequence<Ar> seq(ar);
		ar & x & y;
	}
};
....

or a pair of free functions found through ADL:

[source, c++]
....
auto& serialise(securepath::serialisation::serialiser& s, point const& v);
auto& serialise(securepath::serialisation::deserialiser& s, point& v);
....

`sequence` opens a DER SEQUENCE for the scope of the object. Wire-stable messages
should end with a `trailing_data` member for forward compatibility.

== Tags

`tag.hpp` provides `implicit_tag(n, obj)` and `explicit_tag(n, obj)` for
context-specific tagging, typically to make optional fields distinguishable:

[source, c++]
....
template<typename Ar>
void serialise(Ar& ar) {
	securepath::serialisation::sequence<Ar> seq(ar);
	ar & implicit_tag(0, id) & implicit_tag(1, name);
}
....

== Choice over a typelist

`choice.hpp` serialises one of several message types as a DER CHOICE keyed by
`type_tag`. This is how the protocol messages of key_server and packet_transport are
encoded:

[source, c++]
....
#include <securepath/serialisation/choice.hpp>

using types = securepath::typelist<
	securepath::serialisation::type_tag<request, 1>,
	securepath::serialisation::type_tag<response, 2>>;

auto der = securepath::serialisation::asn_der_serialise_choice<types>(request{...});
....

`packet_deserialiser<types>` (`util.hpp`) feeds received buffers to a visitor whose
`operator()` overloads match the message types; unknown tags are reported instead of
crashing, and a maximum packet size can be given.

== Codec

`codec/asn_der` implements the encoder/decoder pair. The framework is codec-agnostic:
a different wire format only needs a new encoder/decoder implementation.
